d he was firing some a big  pillar of fire at at a hiccup on and and  toothless and and on the storyboard it  was it was neat on the storyboard is  cool you know and an animation is really  neat because just wow the scale came in  but the other end unsung here is this  whole thing I think our effects team and  we were sitting in the back of the  theater watching the movie and watching  for people's reactions and that scene  rolled by and it had been finished and  lit with the  facts and it just was suddenly massive  in this dragon was flying up cutting  through his own pillow fire with his  wing and he's just like oh wow we  screamed really loud I was like you mine  ha the story originally was written  between 1956 in 1959 by the great  filmmaker and French actor Jacques Tati  and tatty has a special place in the  hearts of people who adore French cinema  is sort of an equivalent of a Buster  Keaton or a chaplain and it was a very  iconic figure in in French film and  apparently a show may saw the red this  original story and thought it would have  been a great to be made into an animated  film interesting thing is that tatty  didn't really think of it as a comedy he  really he kind of wrote it as a love  letter to his to one of his daughters  and when when Shan Mei was doing  triplets of belleville in 2003 there was  a scene where some of the characters  were sitting watching television and he  thought it would have been fun to put a  live-action thing on the television  instead of its always human you know  live-action characters watching  animation on TV he'd have been fun to  have a cartoon sitting and watching live  action on television so so at first he  was going to put something from the Tour  de France and then he thought well let  me put some of Monsieur helos holiday in  there and and and the his producer in  the process of getting the rights to use  the the tatty footage befriended the  surviving daughter I knew so Sasha  touchy Jeff and and she granted 